{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "sourceHash": "be7d7b7e07ef60f39957a95334eae46bb61b779a7d4326275bdec5502764768e",
  "notes": [
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c005-b0100",
      "sourceHash": "af5f5dc2520dbe2d5486866310b85c0ac39d9aead5402ec4ad75548cda52b58d",
      "glossaryId": "B01-097",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "In Chinese the name echoes a thousand reds weeping together.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c005-b0104",
      "sourceHash": "44cbb7633f18144fcff7019790efd120ef98a6d969a2974ee2eb596918bae68a",
      "glossaryId": "B01-098",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "In Chinese the name echoes ten thousand beauties sharing grief.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c019-b0005",
      "sourceHash": "4940edc4992e5083089caa070475ce39e208b887ecfbb050dcf7700320d5af93",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-016-020.json#source_treatments:hlm-c019-b0005",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Both [text missing] markers identify lacunae in the chosen Chinese source, not pauses in Bao-yu’s thoughts. The study’s name and the words after his supposition are missing; no wording from another edition has been supplied.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c020-b0023",
      "sourceHash": "114d35f3a731b21a7ba87ba89a79e8944fb79c0ec395e1ac738e29a93d78c2be",
      "glossaryId": "B04-111",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Bao-yu’s saying is translated in the sense he gives it next. Its wording can also be read as “Nearer kin should not separate remoter kin; the earlier should not overstep the later”, which pulls against his explanation.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c020-b0026",
      "sourceHash": "075befe5fea93b76b1cbf814cd4d5a92145a1a6cbfca6d64f713a5eeb550c397",
      "glossaryId": "B04-113",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "The teasing turns on Xiang-yun’s saying ai (“love”) for er (“second”), hence “Dear Cousin” and the altered count. The further sound e joins the teasing at the end of the exchange.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c021-b0010",
      "sourceHash": "ad84ffa25cecac01ebc0adcb084df31a99a0194122eef83d9e3fbefc82067a53",
      "glossaryId": "B05-002",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Hui-xiang means ‘orchid fragrance’; Bao-yu plays on its first syllable with huiqi, ‘bad luck’.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c021-b0012",
      "sourceHash": "a09896702576f201ce12f06fcbbfd9da7bb8b32a74c2cb5a0f76ef24f7e04ece",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-021-025.json#LIT-MECH-01",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "The four objects also allude to names: flower (hua) is Aroma’s surname; musk points to Musk; hairpin (chai) and jade (yu) point to Bao-chai and Dai-yu.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c022-b0054",
      "sourceHash": "7346497fbf587c9e161da9cdaf10166120803ca81b0ab1e7ce872918fdfa5cde",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-021-025.json#LIT-MECH-03",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "In Chinese, lìzhī, “lychee”, echoes lìzhī, “standing on a branch”.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c022-b0060",
      "sourceHash": "391e0951d002aba9e184edae3957eac5fafc765a5f736f19dc83bfb18f949530",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-021-025.json#LIT-MECH-03",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Yán, “speech”, and yàn, “inkstone”, sound alike apart from their tones.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c023-b0030",
      "sourceHash": "56e56ab9b701eaea1c8a0017a4e48dd0711392490645651aa11677bc5a092609",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-021-025.json#LIT-MECH-04",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Musk’s Chinese name contains “moon”, and Sandal’s contains “cloud”. The lines play on both maids’ names.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c023-b0047",
      "sourceHash": "9037d3cf37c461ffa8635b36895c5769432c9ef1cedc155b165c08f6f3388967",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-021-025.json#/literary_boundaries/chapter_023_complete_poems/3/lines/1",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Shuang is short for sushuang, a traditional bird name used here in the name of a sumptuous quilt.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c024-b0013",
      "sourceHash": "93c73e495b20dc56f9f85bb5deb92623b031b249c45c33a36f9651f765ddb67c",
      "glossaryId": "B05-047",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Bu Shi-ren’s name echoes bù shì rén, “not a human being”.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c025-b0024",
      "sourceHash": "8898dfd6640c9c2dead22ff50954e5780eab1cb951b26d76788a79d052872052",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-021-025.json#/scene_risks/chapters_024_025/27",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "The joke turns on tea as a traditional betrothal gift. No betrothal has taken place in this scene.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c026-b0028",
      "sourceHash": "c640e749237a52fc3f4b7b4b5fbe663b625be0c4ffed4c218f2eec32229c5340",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-026-030.json#M03",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Xue Pan misreads the written characters 唐寅 (Tang Yin) as 庚黄 (Geng Huang). Tang Yin also sounds like 糖银, “sugar silver”; Xue Pan follows it with 果银, “fruit silver”.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c027-title",
      "sourceHash": "b956dbd897469d9d2b923971fe0f870a07d51af5e4bb2773295e4c6a40b251a3",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-026-030.json#L-026027-14",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "The heading compares Bao-chai to Noble Consort Yang and Dai-yu to Flying Swallow, two celebrated beauties of earlier dynasties.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c027-b0004",
      "sourceHash": "b70c227a4b34765b9f00aee09471e78020640397f873860d36cad405c7f1d1d4",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-026-030.json#B06-CR2-155",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "The Chinese text lists both Qiao-jie and Da-jie. Both names are retained without deciding whether they refer to separate children.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c028-b0008",
      "sourceHash": "819c11bd48ee8d303635b592e629ce6dca8da1be9dfc4145445fbca712f61d91",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-026-030.json#M08",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Bao and bei together make baobei, “treasure”. Dai-yu takes the bao in Bao-chai’s name and invents a Miss Bei to complete the joke.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c029-b0041",
      "sourceHash": "8e8a6f06b214aa8f2553c5730ec25d5a8288204668315204a92f20fb9a0a037f",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-026-030.json#M21",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Yuanjia can mean an enemy or an adversary, but also a lover who makes one suffer. Grandmother’s reference to a former life adds the sense of an old debt.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c030-b0025",
      "sourceHash": "b7cc1d3309998bdae3befa82abcfbcf0e4aa77d13b0412b718c93ef569b88cce",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-026-030.json#L-030-35",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "In the proverbial story, Dong Shi tries to imitate the beautiful Xi Shi by copying her frown. The word for frowning, pin, also occurs in Dai-yu’s familiar name Pin-pin.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c030-b0026",
      "sourceHash": "bf9c6cf6916e647c950cd632a279aa0953fdf72b087a6dba44b628251ba8c32d",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-026-030.json#M24",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "The text counts eighteen strokes for this character. Its traditional form is 薔, the first character of qiangwei, ‘rose’; the simplified form is 蔷.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c036-b0015",
      "sourceHash": "f6d3f7e0583431812c22d0fdd14035686ca18b0fce4202e1809d593e57e87093",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-036-040.json#/terms/25",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Baixi (白犀), literally ‘white rhinoceros’, is the source’s material name; the precise variety of horn is uncertain.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c036-b0030",
      "sourceHash": "e89fe8ec48506e6a17cdbf7ed692a4654add8d6f96966ec7930ba68db71e5e6b",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-036-040.json#/inheritedApplications/33",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Qiang, the character traced beneath the roses, is also the Qiang in Jia Qiang’s name.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c037-b0003",
      "sourceHash": "c1a22111c7768e5c4dfac92bb275fe29d738eb1e86949770bcb21202b1b71878",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-036-040.json#/literary/letters/0",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Being ‘unfit to gather firewood’ is a conventional way of saying one is ill. The carriage-pins and shafts recall hosts unwilling to let their guests leave.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c037-b0017",
      "sourceHash": "c780551bb11b296080e0bf2d0686678c797d5bcb3bb8039978d39777320c762a",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-036-040.json#/terms/16",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "The prescribed Chinese rhyme-words are mén, ‘door’; pén, ‘pot’; hún, ‘soul’; hén, ‘trace’; and hūn, ‘dusk’. The English retains their images rather than imposing a new rhyme scheme.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c037-b0053",
      "sourceHash": "3cc1879cdefb5d8dfaefeaf4cfe4d3cb0bd44e8bb1241f7bc922168df10df012",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-036-040.json#/terms/29",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Lianzhu, ‘linked pearls’, may describe joined rounded forms or bead-like decoration.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c037-b0067",
      "sourceHash": "91993fe90a4b60b54fedf737a7904d0f0c8191c7dbc278647deb42b18f2b9586",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-036-040.json#/literary/works/4",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Qian-niang is the heroine of a tale in which her soul leaves her body.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c038-b0019",
      "sourceHash": "113578642e36d8fa99f230ce7c59577dfbb3d33025cf1dac6e0812ff36dc0751",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-036-040.json#/terms/13",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Heng (蘅), Xiao (潇), Jiang (绛), Xiang (湘) and Xia (霞) are the characters used to claim subjects. Bao-yu replaces Xiang, from Xiang-yun’s name, with Xia, ‘rosy clouds’, to match her new pen-name.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c038-b0048",
      "sourceHash": "ab40cd5ad443bac40e048bd4d1ff714935a91bddb126d8c63de2c66d64d9c78c",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-036-040.json#/terms/32",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "The three paths, Magistrate Tao and the gentleman of Pengze evoke the poet Tao Yuanming and his life in retirement.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c038-b0061",
      "sourceHash": "d5db95447788eb0387a0bee2b08440c0ecc6af2c4009c75c5cb5489707cc2024",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-036-040.json#/literary/works/13",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Tiaotuo is an old word for a bracelet. Here autumn fragrance rises imaginatively from beneath the painter’s wrist.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c038-b0084",
      "sourceHash": "03c3f99c62fbeab95b67dc9fe6be9beefc9b3ef346fe9e86025cecfe192546da",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-036-040.json#/literary/works/17",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Zhuang’s butterfly recalls Zhuangzi’s dream of being a butterfly, and the question of which was dreaming the other.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c038-b0110",
      "sourceHash": "ec7d347c01a2d7e1da031f0e0eac22f1d19ae517cfcb09845d1aa171b5f55138",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-036-040.json#/literary/works/21",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "‘Spring and Autumn’ evokes the chronicle and its tradition of moral judgement. Hidden judgements become the black and yellow contents of a crab’s shell.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c040-b0044",
      "sourceHash": "3ceb0ec07def99ff82de20529e831f28172c2bdf5ae6f0dde0684b1f5293e3d7",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-036-040.json#/terms/47",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "The rules require a fitting reply that rhymes with the Chinese call. The English keeps the images rather than making new rhyming answers.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c040-b0053",
      "sourceHash": "6b0e0ac32a767122533ba0134f4c0fea3398127be7038c268f6155d37b39c86e",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-036-040.json#/terms/62",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "‘Great Long Five’ and ‘Great Five Long’ name the same tile, as do ‘Long Three’ and ‘Three Long’. Reversing the words changes the Chinese rhyme; the English preserves the reversal.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c040-b0081",
      "sourceHash": "2aa7d025a4d205763b9cfe5890fc31799aed92277274988000c84b925a938558",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-036-040.json#/inheritedApplications/59",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Dai-yu repeats the line from The Peony Pavilion heard in Chapter 23. Its final Chinese word is tian, ‘day’ or ‘heaven’, answering the Heaven tile.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c041-b0016",
      "sourceHash": "dadee53b51045926235c3e5f60298ea3dae9ffb53eb1a7b412d6c608582a0eba",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-041-045.json#/selectedReaderNotes/0",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Banpao is the cup’s inscribed name. The inscription does not establish its material or authenticate its stated history.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c041-b0017",
      "sourceHash": "c279f4507a955b115d4c0ca3653080b0c5ca9a331a22f2574cc1354d7600235e",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-041-045.json#/selectedReaderNotes/1",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Xingxi follows the characters in this Chinese text. The vessel’s material and the meaning of its name are uncertain.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c042-b0004",
      "sourceHash": "119e70906d2fc2212c38a66137b122a7f12f49f9b9a29f92d5e08a546829ca35",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-041-045.json#/selectedReaderNotes/2",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "The girl's protective pet-name uses ge, a boy-style address. Qiao links her seventh-night birthday with the lucky turn Grannie Liu wishes for her.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c042-b0013",
      "sourceHash": "291999c87d5a67ef52bc1265f767df1387d64d4f8a6912c9ea58bdc86fbb5ecf",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-041-045.json#/selectedReaderNotes/3",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "A rebus expressing 'May everything surely go as you wish': 'brush and ingot' sounds like 'certainly'.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c042-b0022",
      "sourceHash": "f2636ee4ea07564a1b0f9c467dab073688f7b7dfd1af79f695365c8c2db553a4",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-041-045.json#/selectedReaderNotes/4",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "The Spring and Autumn Annals was traditionally associated with Confucius and with judgements conveyed in very few words. Bao-chai applies that lofty method to Dai-yu’s pointed joke.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c042-b0031",
      "sourceHash": "3f99c08d0bfdbd06cbe76db58085b94f09dcaabe7bc6f652fa943d6c5bb27579",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-041-045.json#/selectedReaderNotes/5",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Dachi is the source’s designation for the gold leaf. Qingjin can denote a blue painting colour or a kind of gold leaf; its identity here is uncertain. Tie is rendered as ‘packets’, without assuming a fixed number of leaves.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c045-b0001",
      "sourceHash": "d3697946f71e47d53705054286dc4f810d819297216de6de03650c9d9a97b69c",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-041-045.json#/selectedReaderNotes/6",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "The Chinese words for poetry and wet sound alike. Xi-feng makes a joke of not knowing about either wet verses or dry ones.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c045-b0029",
      "sourceHash": "1fc4157ac67003bb81faec2cd92e9d92d016130de10dce7a569a6ec59f17c952",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-041-045.json#/selectedReaderNotes/7",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "In the Analects (12.5), Sima Niu laments that everyone has brothers except him. Zixia replies that a gentleman may find brothers throughout the world.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c045-b0043",
      "sourceHash": "84a2e6e6552fbd116d017cba6437634eec7c4f3af2c36deef7a2d932ddac31b0",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-041-045.json#/selectedReaderNotes/8",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Miansha is the source's yarn designation. The exact fibre intended here is not securely established.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c046-title",
      "sourceHash": "a91a826d0a167e40921794e24ffbb16d07433600f3ba6dc88ee664c9037aebbc",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/0",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Faithful’s Chinese name, Yuanyang, means “mandarin ducks”, a traditional image of a married pair.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c046-b0024",
      "sourceHash": "27f2b20baf2a7e974d9ec4901ffbfe6e131790bd588c9930e53cae885aaf88e7",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/1",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "“Fine words” and “fine pictures” sound alike in Chinese. The pox is of a grade then considered auspicious, with fully formed pustules.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c046-b0038",
      "sourceHash": "7d6d20f4e7f1615e001f184c40b6ae0f2ec1b6ef515edb8c311804390876a067",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/2",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Bao means “precious”: Bao-yu (“precious jade”) is exaggerated into Bao-jin (“precious gold”) and Bao-yin (“precious silver”), then a heavenly king and an emperor.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c047-b0002",
      "sourceHash": "432cf14ac7e6158b0d337f5e586dfdc85ec7033a358a8dc1bc612ae63a089b6e",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/3",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Traditional prescriptions for women: obedience to father, husband and son in turn, and virtue in conduct, speech, appearance and work.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c047-b0010",
      "sourceHash": "436e93c235bd3d8ab453d4abd468e269558665063f1411c3c21b14acae8c3181",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/4",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "A shichen is a traditional division of the day, approximately two modern hours.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c047-b0014",
      "sourceHash": "22240b82f44fd5957567e0316c23e3eb9008d09fc388b64440355387cfd08ac0",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/5",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "The surname Bao sounds like bao, “to hug”; Grandmother follows it with “carrying on the back”.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c048-b0010",
      "sourceHash": "052794752b1b3676adbee2aaac428fbca4e9b8c2d73ccc1f6dec3e6f0e66949a",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/6",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Long and Shu are regions: the saying describes wanting another conquest as soon as one has been won.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c048-b0014",
      "sourceHash": "592b4dfecfaab13186d00260691dae795a30bb6e67d2e3b82e9f2058c65ae2fa",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/7",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Milu is retained as the source’s name for a fan material; its precise identity is uncertain.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c048-b0018",
      "sourceHash": "bc07d41b49ab6f403dd977dcb78ab211e11b820217098d4bacd1766c8018cfe7",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/8",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "The numbers refer to character positions in Chinese lines. Level and oblique are traditional tone categories, not English stresses.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c048-b0020",
      "sourceHash": "dcfe29ff80807b0fa5bd997ebc4d672ac87ed48e5391f356c1c940a3f56f8191",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/9",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Wang Mo-jie and Wang You-cheng are names for Wang Wei; old Du is Du Fu; Li Qing-lian is Li Bai; Lu Fang-weng is Lu You.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c048-b0050",
      "sourceHash": "38798c60d1e31f89cd0abc499fc7e3dbe84fd04b97b0cf967931ce352ca31caa",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/10",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Han and shan are the fourteenth and fifteenth groups in the traditional rhyme tables. Xian, “leisure”, belongs to shan: Caltrop has heard the invitation to rest as a suggested rhyme.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c049-b0009",
      "sourceHash": "dc8daceef2f5dd0006c0215ba980a03838ed4f0394f0a5a79f8cb64610ceb5ac",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/11",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Chang-e is the lady of the moon.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c049-b0025",
      "sourceHash": "5dbd16069d527c93874233818fee489cfc0b91d1fded4b3462a4b51a5ba40514",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/12",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Du of the Board of Works is Du Fu; Wei of Suzhou is Wei Ying-wu; Wen Ba-cha is Wen Ting-yun; Li Yi-shan is Li Shang-yin.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c049-b0033",
      "sourceHash": "d45ddc631748fe304bba8d40e34920b0258a9b647e29c1e53a1cdf6760d3ca87",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/13",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Meng Guang and Liang Hong are a proverbial devoted couple. The clause quoted in Chinese has seven characters, although the source calls them five; its three-character question is rendered “When was it that”.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c049-b0041",
      "sourceHash": "21e2d246ff1018502295fc3d8c995709ff3f0caea23d1cb975d391ebcc9ad2be",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/14",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Several historical textile and dress names are retained in these descriptions. Dou does not specify an identifiable pattern shape, nor zhezi an exact cut; “sea-dragon”, “jade-needle” and “golden-vine” do not establish modern material identifications.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c050-b0183",
      "sourceHash": "16e695650eab7fc5d36af59fe0fe38a76bad0a8ac5c77eaae1ec67bb352ac986",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/15",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Guanyin’s goodness and the lack of a recorded biography supply the two parts of the answer. Both guesses are phrases from the Four Books.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c050-b0187",
      "sourceHash": "0c7a9e5a850e0f37ecf8db88f2616180eab9c6a6bb823e622dadc7270afce940",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/16",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Shan Tao’s name means “mountain” and “wave”, answering the stone and water in the clue.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c050-b0188",
      "sourceHash": "3339b66655f2af6da7e11aac3011acb8f3e333c0d1f2da6d3fbc8d8f84d075fa",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-046-050.json#/selectedReaderNotes/17",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "The character hua (花, “flower”) combines the grass component with hua (化, “change”). Dai-yu invokes the traditional belief that fireflies arose from decaying grass.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c051-b0053",
      "sourceHash": "414548011d7682563471e6f82b9c75be6f434e4b928872d0260a1db1ad99a945",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-051-055.json#/selectedReaderNotes/0",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "The image is of fixing the movable bridges of a zither so that it can no longer be tuned: a figure for rigidly applying a rule.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c052-b0018",
      "sourceHash": "c74e1b413a38e427e0fda81222249ee5bf166b78d9c6dce72beceb80f0312b65",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-051-055.json#/selectedReaderNotes/1",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "A five-character regulated poem has only eight lines. The traditional xian rhyme group contains far more rhyming words than such a poem could use: Bao-chai’s demand is a joke, not a workable rule.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c054-b0011",
      "sourceHash": "336f88b4808bf89561ce2d610bc0c87a23d21500c6978084dd95f6c6ed016fe5",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-051-055.json#/selectedReaderNotes/2",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Faithful’s surname is Jin, “gold”, and Aroma’s is Hua, “flower”. Ripple’s theatrical joke combines their surnames.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c054-b0024",
      "sourceHash": "e07bd2330bd8f14051bd0b841d4b7d29a68e698a662f42e08da9082b8a5dee03",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-051-055.json#/selectedReaderNotes/3",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "The allusion is to an exemplary filial son entertaining his aged parents in brightly patterned children’s clothes. Xi-feng turns the example into a playful defence of making the old lady laugh.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c055-b0018",
      "sourceHash": "0b31e3fb30495e913b3d28579898fcbec93caac69d84959298c98b9ebafe353c",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-051-055.json#/selectedReaderNotes/4",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Tan-chun is formally reckoned as the principal wife’s daughter, although Aunt Zhao is her birth mother. Her claim about her uncle follows that distinction.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c058-b0015",
      "sourceHash": "f0a6964b74b34d28c967727a97e062c2050552b1895e0ea9fd8650de49794331",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-056-060.json#/selectedReaderNotes/0",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "In the Chinese line, fruit also suggests children. Bao-yu links the fruit-laden tree to Xiu-yan's future marriage.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c058-b0017",
      "sourceHash": "58d2d21773e7bc1e4b5be1ee4a24252aaa52f5e0a80628a7fa8e691f3415f691",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-056-060.json#/selectedReaderNotes/1",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Gongye Chang, a disciple of Confucius, was said in legend to understand the language of birds.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    },
    {
      "unitId": "hlm-c060-b0017",
      "sourceHash": "1917f6075c34038fa011407e5a7eb6c7cdf7436ad2fb774ab7492291c3d0c483",
      "glossaryId": "english-batch-056-060.json#/selectedReaderNotes/2",
      "type": "translator_note",
      "text": "Mei-xiang is a conventional maid's name. The saying means that becoming sworn sisters does not change the women's status as servants.",
      "presentationStatus": "not_integrated_in_reading_presentation"
    }
  ]
}
